====Usage notes====
In isolation, the character {{lang|ja|母}} has 5 strokes in modern Japanese – it is not simplified. In {{l|ja|新字体|sc=Latn|shinjitai}} compound characters, such as {{ja-l|毎}} or {{ja-l|海}}, it is simplified to 4 strokes, as {{ja-l|毋}}. In {{l|ja|表外字|sc=Latn|hyōgaiji}} characters such as {{ja-l|姆}}, however, the component is not simplified.

====Alternative forms====
* {{ja-l|嚊}}, {{ja-l|嬶}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|ja|haha}}
{{ja-kanjitab|yomi=k|はは}}

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=はは|-}} ''papa'',<ref name="KDJc">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten Concise}}</ref> in turn traced to {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|*papa}}.

{{IPAchar|/haha/}}: *{{IPAchar|/papa/}} → {{IPAchar|/fafa/}} → {{IPAchar|/fawa/}} → {{IPAchar|/fafa/}} → {{IPAchar|/haha/}}.

First cited to a portion of the ''{{w|Shoku Nihongi}}'' dated to 729, with the phonetic spelling {{m|ja||婆婆|tr=papa}}.<ref name="KDJc"/>

Medial {{IPAchar|/f/}} {{IPAchar|[ɸ]}} regularly changes to {{IPAchar|/w/}} {{IPAchar|[ɰᵝ]}}, resulting in {{IPAchar|/fawa/}} → {{IPAchar|/hawa/}}, the expected final form; see ''hawa'' below. This form first appears in the {{w|Heian period}}. However, likely due to spelling influence or reduplication associations, the earlier {{IPAchar|/fafa/}} resurfaced in the late 16th century towards the end of the {{w|Muromachi period}}, with both forms seen until recent times, when ''hawa'' falls into disuse.<ref name="KDJc"/> Initial {{IPAchar|/f-/}} {{IPAchar|[ɸ]}} regularly becomes {{IPAchar|[h]}}, resulting in modern {{IPAchar|[ha̠ha̠]}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|はは|acc=1|acc_ref=DJR,NHK}}

====Noun====
{{ja-noun|はは|hhira=はは}}

# [[mother]]
#: {{ja-x|織%田%信%長の'''母'''は謎が多い。|^お%だ% ^のぶ%なが の '''はは''' は なぞ が おおい。|Oda Nobunaga's '''mother''' was a mysterious woman.}}
#* {{RQ:Wamyō Ruijushō|1|116|passage=母 尔雅云、母爲妣、卑履反、去聲之重、和名'''波々'''、日本紀私記云、以路波}}
#* {{RQ:Nippo Jisho|196|passage='''Fafa'''. l, faua. ハハ. または、ハワ(母) 母.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* This term conveys neither positive nor negative connotations. However, using it to describe someone the speaker knows personally is often considered lacking respect, where more polite forms like {{ja-r|お母さん|おかあさん}} are preferred.
* This term is sometimes used in objective narrations, but for this purpose, {{ja-r|母%親|はは%おや}} is more common.

